issue.body:
This PR makes it so that ESLint correctly warns on computed methods for the `object-shorthand` method. The rule will now warn for the following when `object-shorthand` is set to `methods` or `always`:

``` js
var foo = {
  [bar]: function() {}
}
```

It continues not to warn in instances where the function is named, and never warns for anything related to computed properties.

I didn't see a sensible place for me to insert this update in the docs, but it's pretty minor so I wasn't sure it really warranted one anyways.
